WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.993
Begin by framing Course Scope and Faculty
Flexibility as a question about inputs,

2
00:00:07.993 --> 00:00:10.437
decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:10.437 --> 00:00:15.306
Specific course details (such as
prerequisites, course seminar, format,

4
00:00:15.306 --> 00:00:21.799
learning outcomes, assessment methods, and
resource needs) will be determined by the

5
00:00:21.799 --> 00:00:27.750
faculty member(s) who propose a specific
seminar course in this area.

6
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:35.727
Consider this worked case: A faculty member
might propose a seminar focused on AI ethics,

7
00:00:35.727 --> 00:00:42.639
while another might focus on computer vision,
each setting their own prerequisites and

8
00:00:42.639 --> 00:00:43.703
assessment methods.

9
00:00:43.703 --> 00:00:50.673
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

10
00:00:50.673 --> 00:00:51.171
conclusion.

11
00:00:51.171 --> 00:00:57.109
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

12
00:00:57.218 --> 00:01:04.647
Now define NIST AI Risk Management Framework:
Govern precisely enough that another learner

13
00:01:04.647 --> 00:01:06.933
could test the definition.

14
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:14.594
The NIST AI Risk Management Framework
organizes work around Govern, Map, Measure,

15
00:01:14.594 --> 00:01:15.871
and Manage.

16
00:01:15.871 --> 00:01:23.167
Consider this worked case: Govern establishes
policies, responsibilities, and accountability

17
00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:30.464
for AI risk, ensuring clear ownership of risk
management decisions.

18
00:01:30.464 --> 00:01:37.041
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

19
00:01:37.041 --> 00:01:39.433
evidence would distinguish them.

20
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:46.121
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

21
00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:54.958
With the foundation in place, examine how NIST
AI Risk Management Framework: Map operates in

22
00:01:54.958 --> 00:01:56.700
a concrete sequence.

23
00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:03.778
Map documents context, intended use, affected
people, and potential impacts.

24
00:02:03.778 --> 00:02:10.974
Consider this worked case: A team maps a
facial recognition system by documenting its

25
00:02:10.974 --> 00:02:17.656
intended use in public spaces, the affected
population, and potential impacts on privacy

26
00:02:17.656 --> 00:02:18.684
and bias.

27
00:02:18.684 --> 00:02:25.918
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

28
00:02:25.918 --> 00:02:31.590
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

29
00:02:31.706 --> 00:02:39.359
Next, compare the choices involved in NIST AI
Risk Management Framework: Measure and make

30
00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:41.546
the decision criteria explicit.

31
00:02:41.546 --> 00:02:48.718
Measure uses qualitative and quantitative
methods to analyze identified risks.

32
00:02:48.718 --> 00:02:56.487
Consider this worked case: A team measures
risk by running quantitative accuracy tests on

33
00:02:56.487 --> 00:03:02.037
training data and conducting qualitative
interviews with stakeholders to assess

34
00:03:02.037 --> 00:03:03.702
fairness and transparency.

35
00:03:03.702 --> 00:03:10.761
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

36
00:03:10.761 --> 00:03:11.937
the goalposts.

37
00:03:11.937 --> 00:03:18.968
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

38
00:03:19.082 --> 00:03:25.815
Apply NIST AI Risk Management Framework:
Manage to a worked situation instead of

39
00:03:25.815 --> 00:03:28.922
treating it as an isolated definition.

40
00:03:28.922 --> 00:03:36.078
Manage prioritizes risks and allocates
resources for response and monitoring.

41
00:03:36.078 --> 00:03:43.367
Consider this worked case: A team prioritizes
high-risk items, allocates budget for

42
00:03:43.367 --> 00:03:50.656
monitoring, and schedules regular audits to
track system behavior and context changes.

43
00:03:50.657 --> 00:03:58.054
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

44
00:03:58.054 --> 00:03:58.547
step.

45
00:03:58.547 --> 00:04:03.422
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

46
00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:09.309
Examine the tradeoffs in Machine-Learning
Workflow Separation, including what improves

47
00:04:09.309 --> 00:04:12.198
and what becomes more difficult.

48
00:04:12.198 --> 00:04:18.938
Machine-learning workflows separate data
preparation, training, validation, testing,

49
00:04:18.938 --> 00:04:22.308
model selection, and evaluation.

50
00:04:22.308 --> 00:04:29.214
Consider this worked case: A workflow
separates raw data cleaning from model

51
00:04:29.214 --> 00:04:35.545
training, ensuring that validation and testing
sets remain untouched during hyperparameter

52
00:04:35.545 --> 00:04:36.120
tuning.

53
00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:43.073
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

54
00:04:43.073 --> 00:04:50.370
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

55
00:04:50.474 --> 00:04:58.073
Stress-test the reasoning around Supervised
vs. Unsupervised Methods with an edge case and

56
00:04:58.073 --> 00:05:01.580
identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

57
00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:07.455
Supervised methods learn a mapping from
examples paired with target labels.

58
00:05:07.455 --> 00:05:17.010
Consider this worked case: A supervised method
classifies emails as spam or not using labeled

59
00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:23.380
data, whereas an unsupervised method clusters
customer behavior without predefined

60
00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:24.017
categories.

61
00:05:24.017 --> 00:05:30.564
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

62
00:05:30.564 --> 00:05:37.705
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

63
00:05:37.826 --> 00:05:45.429
Use Preprocessing and Leakage Prevention to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

64
00:05:45.429 --> 00:05:47.963
causes, and corrective action.

65
00:05:47.963 --> 00:05:54.791
Preprocessing must be fitted only on permitted
training data to avoid leakage.

66
00:05:54.791 --> 00:06:01.716
Consider this worked case: A team fits a
scaling transformer only on the training

67
00:06:01.716 --> 00:06:08.640
split, then applies the same transformation to
validation and testing data to prevent data

68
00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:09.135
leakage.

69
00:06:09.135 --> 00:06:16.894
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

70
00:06:16.894 --> 00:06:18.557
smallest plausible cause.

71
00:06:18.557 --> 00:06:25.744
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

72
00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:32.976
Connect Pipelines and Cross-Validation to the
earlier ideas and explain how the pieces work

73
00:06:32.976 --> 00:06:34.503
as one system.

74
00:06:34.503 --> 00:06:41.612
Cross-validation repeats training and
validation across defined data splits.

75
00:06:41.612 --> 00:06:49.982
Consider this worked case: A pipeline ensures
that scaling and imputation are applied

76
00:06:49.982 --> 00:06:55.133
identically to every fold during
cross-validation, preventing inconsistent

77
00:06:55.133 --> 00:06:57.065
transformations across splits.

78
00:06:57.065 --> 00:07:06.627
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

79
00:07:06.627 --> 00:07:08.221
across representations.

80
00:07:08.221 --> 00:07:15.815
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

81
00:07:15.938 --> 00:07:22.259
Finish with Evaluation Metrics and Practical
Analysis Checklist as a practical checklist

82
00:07:22.259 --> 00:07:25.419
that can guide a new analysis.

83
00:07:25.419 --> 00:07:31.731
Evaluation metrics must match the task and the
cost of different errors.

84
00:07:31.731 --> 00:07:39.559
Consider this worked case: A team selects a
metric like F1-score for an imbalanced

85
00:07:39.559 --> 00:07:45.150
classification task, ensuring that
hyperparameter tuning does not influence the

86
00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.387
final test set evaluation.

87
00:07:47.388 --> 00:07:55.228
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

88
00:07:55.228 --> 00:07:56.434
verification step.

89
00:07:56.434 --> 00:08:03.216
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

90
00:08:03.216 --> 00:08:03.700
intuition.
